```

# DESCRIPTION

Plan 9's file calls, on the process's namespace (09 §5.5, M6 step 6e4d1).
A `vx_fd` is an index and a generation into the process's table of 256 open files, so a closed one's number finds nothing; `VX_STDIN`, `VX_STDOUT` and `VX_STDERR` (0, 1 and 2) are the standard streams.
A call that cannot returns a negative `vx_status`, and `vx_errstr` (arena(2)) says what and why.
The table is safe between threads: a file's reads and writes hold its slot, so threads on different files do not wait on each other.

`vx_open` opens <path> with <mode>: `VX_OREAD`, `VX_OWRITE`, `VX_ORDWR` or `VX_OEXEC`, with `VX_OTRUNC` to empty it, `VX_ORCLOSE` to remove it when closed, and `VX_OAPPEND` for every write to go to its end (the server's own append where it has one, 9Px's posix extension).
`vx_create` makes the file with permissions <perm>, a directory with `VX_DMDIR`; a file already there is emptied and opened, as Plan 9's `create` does, unless <mode> has `VX_OEXCL`, which makes it `VX_ERR_EXISTS`: what an atomic write needs.

`vx_read` and `vx_write` work at the file's offset and move it on; one call is one request, so fewer bytes than asked is no error, and a read of 0 is the end.
`vx_pread` and `vx_pwrite` work at <off> and leave the offset alone.
`vx_seek` moves it, from the start, where it is or the end (`VX_SEEK_SET`, `VX_SEEK_CUR`, `VX_SEEK_END`), and returns where it is.

`vx_stat` says what <path> is in a `vx_dir`: its name, owner, group and last writer (in arena <a>), its `qid` and `dev` (two names are one file when their `dev` and `qid.path` agree), its mode, length, and access and change times as nanoseconds of UTC since 1970 (seconds, made nanoseconds, where the server keeps no more).
Symbolic links are followed, in the client, as the servers walk names only; `vx_lstat` looks at a link itself (`VX_DMSYMLINK` in its mode).
`vx_wstat` changes what <d> sets: start from `vx_dir_keep`, whose values change nothing. A change of name or owner is a 9P `wstat`; mode, length and times alone go by `setattr` where the server has it, which keeps nanoseconds.
`vx_dirread` reads a directory's entries, all of them from where its reading is, into an array in <a>.

`vx_rename` moves <from> to <to>, replacing what is there, across directories of one server; across servers it is `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`, and the caller copies.
`vx_symlink` makes a link at <path> to <target>, and `vx_readlink` reads one; there are no hard links (decided 2026-10-05).
`vx_sync` returns once what was written to <fd> is on stable storage.

`vx_map` maps <len> bytes of the file from <off>, a page multiple: its server's own pages (`Tmap`, 01 §5), shared, so what is written to them is the file's. <prot> is `VX_MAP_WRITE`, `VX_MAP_EXEC` (never both: W^X) or 0 for read-only. A server without `Tmap` gives `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED`.
`vx_unmap` lets go of a mapping.

`vx_ctl` writes one formatted message to <path> in one write: how a program speaks to a server's `ctl` file.

The same requests go through a loop (loop(2)) without a second API (M6 step 6e4d2, F-217).
`vx_io_submit` takes <n> requests, each a `vx_io`: a file, an operation (`VX_IO_READ`, `VX_IO_WRITE` or `VX_IO_SYNC`), an offset (requests are positioned, as `vx_pread`'s), a buffer that stays the caller's until it completes, and a key.
It takes all of them or none, `VX_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT` when the loop has no room for them all (256 at once).
Each completes as a `VX_EV_IO` with its key and its count, bytes or a negative `vx_status`, in the order the server answers.
In level 1 a loop's requests are made by up to four I/O threads of its own, the pipelined client keeping each in flight; the flags, cache hints for the server, are not yet carried.

`vx_watch` makes changes to a file, or to a directory's entries, `VX_EV_CHANGED` on <l> with <key>, until the loop is freed: what changed, and in a directory the entry's name (9Px `notify`, `docs/proto/notify.md`; fsd, tmpfs and srvfs serve it).
On `VX_STDIN` it gives one `VX_EV_READY` once there is input to read; a program reads it and watches again.

# EXAMPLES

Writing a file atomically, as Foundation does:

```c
vx_fd fd = vx_create(VX_STR("/tmp/x.new"), VX_OWRITE | VX_OEXCL, 0644);
if (fd < 0) return vx_errstr();
vx_write(fd, data);
vx_sync(fd);
vx_close(fd);
vx_rename(VX_STR("/tmp/x.new"), VX_STR("/tmp/x"));
```

